# Quantum Darwinism Beyond Redundancy Counting

## Toward a Quantitative Scaling Law for Environment-Induced Information Replication

### Part I — Foundations, Formal Setting, and the Redundancy Scaling Problem

---

# Abstract

Quantum Darwinism proposes that classical objectivity emerges because environments do not merely decohere quantum systems—they selectively proliferate information about preferred observables into many environmental fragments. Independent observers then infer an effectively objective reality by intercepting redundant environmental records rather than directly measuring the system.

Although Quantum Darwinism explains why pointer states become objectively accessible, no general quantitative law presently determines how redundancy scales with environmental size, coupling structure, observer precision, and environmental complexity. Existing formulations define redundancy operationally through mutual-information thresholds but stop short of deriving a universal scaling relation.

This work introduces a quantitative program for redundancy growth and proposes a formal scaling theory. We construct an information-geometric description of environment-mediated replication and derive a candidate General Redundancy Scaling Law (GRSL), relating observable redundancy to environmental dimensionality, complexity, correlation structure, and decoherence flow.

Part I establishes the mathematical framework and derives the governing variables.

---

